# Narration for matched company decision-graph rules.
surge-0-0: [A/P/E] The crowd exceeds fixed capacity. Requests queue, viewers give up, and emergency capacity costs more than the quiet-week saving.
surge-0-1: [{Api}] The crowd reaches {Endpoint} on a single-zone server. Its small serving capacity queues requests; emergency capacity and refunds consume the quiet-week saving.
surge-0-2: [A/P/E] The crowd exceeds fixed capacity. Requests queue, viewers give up, and emergency capacity costs more than the quiet-week saving.
surge-bounded-invoice: [P] Every request gets capacity, including the retry storm. The service stays up; the scaling invoice does not have a comfortable ceiling.
surge-1-0: [P] Every request gets capacity, including the retry storm. The service stays up; the scaling invoice does not have a comfortable ceiling.
surge-2-0: [A/P/E] Bounded scaling and edge delivery absorb the crowd. The load test's limits finally earn their keep.
leak-3-0: [I] A deployment credential with broad permissions escapes. The attacker reads account data and starts a mining fleet. Response and credits consume the emergency fund.
leak-4-0: [{Data}] A shared credential escapes from the public database holding {Record}. An attacker follows the same reachable backend path as {Endpoint} and copies customer data.
leak-5-0: [O] Full request logs contain reusable credentials. A leaked log export becomes an account-access incident.
leak-6-0: [E/S] Public object URLs expose the licensed catalogue without checking payment. The content is copied; partners demand takedowns and compensation.
leak-7-0: [I/E/D/O] The attacker finds no usable credential or exposed data path. Private access and redaction contain this attempt.
archive-8-0: [S] The live catalogue is in Deep Archive. The advertised release needs an asynchronous restore, not an instant GET. Retrieval work and credits swallow the storage saving.
archive-9-0: [S] Fans reread the hot catalogue all weekend. Standard-IA retrieval fees overtake the lower storage price.
archive-10-0: [E->S] The client requests an S3 object, but the content is on EBS. No compatible serving origin was configured.
archive-11-0: [S] The active objects are available when requested. The catalogue opens without a restore queue.
zone-12-0: [S/A/D] An AZ disruption takes the single-zone component with it. Other healthy services cannot complete the request path.
zone-12-1: [{Api}] An AZ disruption takes the server running {Endpoint} with it. Healthy services elsewhere cannot complete this API path.
zone-12-2: [{Data}] An AZ disruption takes the single-zone backend holding {Record} offline. {Endpoint} cannot complete its requests.
zone-13-0: [S/A/D] Redundant components carry the AZ failure. A regional plan would address a different, larger failure.
restore-14-0: [R] The primary Region is unavailable and backups are local. Rebuilding from offline sources takes more than a week.
restore-15-0: [R] The copies exist, but recovery routing was never tested. An IAM permission blocks the runbook.
restore-16-0: [R] The cross-Region recovery rehearsal pays off. The correct data and routing permissions are ready.
bill-17-0: [O] Full payload logging grows with traffic and retains secrets. The log-ingestion invoice arrives before anyone checks the retention policy.
bill-18-0: [P] Peak capacity runs through a quiet week. Reserved headroom cannot be used to pay salaries; the recurring bill still comes due.
bill-19-0: [O] A retry loop goes unnoticed without service or billing alarms. Budget notifications would not cap spending, but would have raised the alarm earlier.
bill-20-0: [O] A spending alert leads to a retry-loop fix before the bill grows. The alert itself is not a spending cap.
jobs-21-0: [W] The forty-minute encode exceeds a standard Lambda invocation. The premiere arrives before its video does.
jobs-22-0: [W] The long-running media job finishes in a service built for it. The API is free to handle short requests.
database-23-0: [{Data}] A shared credential escapes. The public backend holding {Record} turns the mistake into a data breach affecting {Endpoint}.
database-24-0: [D] The entitlement lookup remains private and available. A small worker mistake does not grant access to the customer table.
partner-public-catalogue: The partner is upset, and brings a link instead of an accusation. [E -> S] The delivery path exposes licensed media without checking entitlement. Paying for a licence did not give us permission to distribute anonymous download links. Repair the delivery access policy before the next release.
partner-archived-premiere: The partner is upset about the missed premiere. [E -> S] CloudFront requests a live title from Deep Archive, which needs an asynchronous restore. The cache cannot conjure a film that has not been restored. Move the live catalogue to an immediately accessible tier.
partner-job-timeout: The partner is upset about an undelivered release. [{Api} -> {Worker}] A short API hands a long-running job to a standard Lambda invocation. Its execution limit interrupts the work. Keep the short API, but move long jobs to suitable workers.
